1
00:03:14,482 --> 00:03:20,482
Pioneer. Leader. Father. Champion.

2
00:06:28,965 --> 00:06:34,965
McLaren is a documentary film directed by Roger Donaldson.

3
00:09:43,448 --> 00:09:49,448
The story of New Zealander Bruce McLaren, who founded the McLaren Motor Racing team, showing the
world that a man of humble beginnings could take on the elite of motor racing and win.

4
00:12:57,931 --> 00:13:03,931
Bruce McLaren, played by Dwayne Cameron.

5
00:16:12,413 --> 00:16:18,413
Self (archive footage), played by Bruce McLaren.

6
00:19:26,896 --> 00:19:32,896
Self, played by Mario Andretti.

7
00:22:41,379 --> 00:22:47,379
Self, played by Alastair Caldwell.

8
00:25:55,862 --> 00:26:01,862
Self, played by Jackie Stewart.

9
00:29:10,344 --> 00:29:16,344
Self, played by Dan Gurney.

10
00:32:24,827 --> 00:32:30,827
Bruce Leslie McLaren (30 August 1937 – 2 June 1970) was a New Zealand racing driver, automotive
designer, engineer and motorsport executive, who competed in Formula One from 1958 to 1970.

11
00:35:39,310 --> 00:35:45,310
McLaren was runner-up in the Formula One World Drivers' Championship in
1960 with Cooper, and won four Grands Prix across 13 seasons.

12
00:38:53,793 --> 00:38:59,793
In endurance racing, McLaren won the 24 Hours of Le Mans in 1966 with Ford.

13
00:42:08,275 --> 00:42:14,275
He founded McLaren in 1963, who have since won 10 Formula One World Constructors' Championship
titles and remain the only team to have completed the Triple Crown of Motorsport.

14
00:45:22,758 --> 00:45:28,758
Born and raised in Auckland, McLaren initially studied engineering at the University
of Auckland before dropping out to focus on his motor racing career.

15
00:48:37,241 --> 00:48:43,241
Having entered his first hillclimbing event aged 14, he progressed to Formula Two
in 1957, winning the New Zealand Championship the following year.

16
00:51:51,724 --> 00:51:57,724
His performance at the New Zealand Grand Prix attracted the attention of Jack Brabham, with whom he partnered at Cooper in
1959 having already debuted at the 1958 German Grand Prix, where he finished fifth in his Formula Two machinery.

17
00:55:06,206 --> 00:55:12,206
Aged 22, McLaren took his maiden win at the United States Grand Prix, becoming the
then-youngest driver to win a Formula One Grand Prix, a record which stood for 44 years.

18
00:58:20,689 --> 00:58:26,689
Remaining at Cooper for 1960, McLaren took a further win in Argentina—amongst several
podiums—as he finished championship runner-up to teammate Brabham.

19
01:01:35,172 --> 01:01:41,172
After a winless 1961 season for Cooper, McLaren won the 1962 Monaco Grand Prix,
finishing third in the championship to Graham Hill and Jim Clark.

20
01:04:49,655 --> 01:04:55,655
Cooper struggled for performance from 1963 to 1965 as Lotus, BRM and Ferrari dominated the
championship, prompting McLaren to enter Formula One with his own team.

21
01:08:04,137 --> 01:08:10,137
McLaren founded Bruce McLaren Motor Racing in 1963, with whom
he competed from 1966 until his death in 1970.

22
01:11:18,620 --> 01:11:24,620
With the team, he won the Belgian Grand Prix in 1968 and finished
third in the 1969 World Drivers' Championship.

23
01:14:33,103 --> 01:14:39,103
In June 1970, he died while testing the McLaren M8D at Goodwood, having
achieved four wins, three fastest laps and 27 podiums in Formula One.

24
01:17:47,586 --> 01:17:53,586
Outside of Formula One, McLaren competed in nine editions of the 24 Hours of Le Mans from
1959 to 1969, winning in 1966 alongside Chris Amon in the Ford GT40 Mk II.

25
01:21:02,068 --> 01:21:08,068
He was also a two-time champion of the Canadian-American Challenge Cup in 1967 and
1969, driving his own M6A and M8B, and won the Tasman Series in 1964.

26
01:24:16,551 --> 01:24:22,551
His legacy has been cemented with the McLaren Group, whose achievements have included winning ten World
Constructors' Championships, two Indianapolis 500s, and the 24 Hours of Le Mans in 1995.

27
01:27:31,034 --> 01:27:37,034
McLaren was inducted into the International Motorsports Hall of Fame in 1991.

28
01:30:45,517 --> 01:30:51,517
The film explores themes of car driving, sports
documentary, rise to fame, dedicated, dedication.
